<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cumberland</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cumberland is a historic county in north-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a> and the Lake District, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> as its chief town. Once part of the British kingdom of Strathclyde, it was disputed between Scottish and English kings: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> held it during the English civil war of Stephen and Matilda, until Henry II recovered it in 1157.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cumberland.jpg" alt="Cumberland" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Michael Walsh · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skiddaw_-_panoramio_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Cumberland is an area of North West England that was historically a county. It was bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, County Durham, Westmorland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a>, and by the Scottish counties of Dumfriesshire and Roxburghshire to the north. It included the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, part of the Lake District and the North Pennines, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solway-firth">Solway Firth</a> coastline, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> to the west. Its highest point, Scafell Pike at 978 metres, is the highest mountain in England, and Carlisle is the county town. The county's boundaries, formed in the twelfth century, were not changed substantially during its existence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Early Middle Ages the region was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-strathclyde">Kingdom of Strathclyde</a> in the Hen Ogledd, the Old North, and its people spoke the Brittonic language now called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a>. The first record of the name Cumberland is in 945, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxon-chronicle">Anglo-Saxon Chronicle</a> says King Edmund I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> ceded the area to Malcolm I, king of Alba. The names Cumberland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a> derive, like the Welsh name Cymru, from a Common Brittonic word meaning compatriots. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domesday-book">Domesday Book</a> was compiled in 1086, most of the future county belonged to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>.</p><p>In 1092 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ii-of-england">William Rufus</a> invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> district, settled it with colonists and granted it to Ranulf le Meschin as an earldom. Carlisle became the see of a new diocese in 1133. After Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> died in 1135, King David I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> regained the area and made Carlisle one of his chief seats of government while England fell into civil war. Henry II took it back in 1157 and formed two counties from the earldom, Westmorland and Carliol, which by 1177 was known as Cumberland. The border between England and Scotland was fixed permanently by the Treaty of York in 1237. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbric">Cumbric</a> is believed to have died out in the twelfth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Cumberland had an administrative role from the twelfth century until 1974, when it was merged with Westmorland and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lancashire">Lancashire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumbria">Cumbria</a>. Local government had been reshaped in the nineteenth century, and a county council was created in 1889. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a> became a county borough in 1914.</p><p>The name survives in Cumberland sausages and in several newspapers and organisations, and in Macbeth it is the kingdom given to Prince Malcolm. A flag based on the arms of the former county council was registered as the flag of Cumberland in 2012, and in 2013 the government recognised the continued existence of the historic counties. A unitary authority named Cumberland, covering most of the historic county but not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penrith">Penrith</a>, was created in 2023.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cumberland” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bragg's Confederate Army of Tennessee and Rosecrans's Union Army of the <b>Cumberland</b> began a three-day battle near Murfreesboro, Tennessee, with the highest casualty rate of the war's major battles.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-31">31 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Jefferson signed the act for a road from <b>Cumberland</b>, Maryland, to Ohio, the first great federally financed highway of the United States.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-29">29 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>British reinforcements ended a siege of Fort <b>Cumberland</b> by Patriot militia under Jonathan Eddy.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-29">29 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Elizabeth II opened Calder Hall in <b>Cumberland</b>, the world's first nuclear power station to supply electricity to a national grid on a commercial scale.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-17">17 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At York, Henry III of England and Alexander II of Scotland agreed that Northumberland, <b>Cumberland</b> and Westmorland were subject to English sovereignty.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-25">25 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
